<p>A <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?p">-value of 2.9% means that t if the effect were exactly zero, roughly 3 in every 100 studies like yours would produce data this extreme or more so. It says nothing about whether the effect is zero.</p>
<p>The histogram visualizes 10,000 experiments conducted in a world where <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?H_0"> is true. The red shaded tail represents the proportion of simulated trials that produced a statistic at or beyond our observed value <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?t_%7B%5Ctext%7Bobs%7D%7D">. The area of that tail is the p-value: a tail probability under an assumed null model, not the probability that the null model itself is correct.</p>
</section>
<section id="a-brief-historical-accident" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="a-brief-historical-accident">A Brief Historical Accident</h2>
<p>When Ronald Fisher introduced the 0.05 threshold in his 1925 classic Statistical Methods for Research Workers, he intended it as an informal, practical benchmark for identifying anomalies worthy of a second look—partly to save researchers from having to interpolate across extensive printed tables. He never conceived it as an absolute dividing line between truth and falsehood, nor as a posterior measure of scientific certainty.</p>
</section>
<section id="takeaway" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="takeaway">Takeaway</h2>
<p>A p-value tells you how surprising your data would be if nothing were happening; it never tells you the probability that nothing is happening.</p>
</section>
